1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,350
A dozen eggs.

2
00:00:01,633 --> 00:00:03,554
One Safeway store in Washington.

3
00:00:03,804 --> 00:00:07,586
Shoppers ordering them through the same app, in the same test, saw

4
00:00:07,586 --> 00:00:08,847
five different prices.

5
00:00:09,212 --> 00:00:10,036
Same eggs.

6
00:00:10,386 --> 00:00:11,236
Same store.

7
00:00:11,471 --> 00:00:13,236
The cheapest was three ninety-nine.

8
00:00:13,564 --> 00:00:15,838
The most expensive was four seventy-nine.

9
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:18,670
That's what people now call surveillance pricing.

10
00:00:18,963 --> 00:00:21,023
A price that depends on who's looking.

11
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:24,874
And in August, the Federal Trade Commission put in writing where it

12
00:00:24,874 --> 00:00:26,440
thinks that crosses the line.

13
00:00:26,725 --> 00:00:30,006
So by the end of this, you'll know what's documented, what's a

14
00:00:30,006 --> 00:00:33,497
myth, and four quick checks that tell you whether the price you're

15
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:34,555
seeing is the price.

16
00:00:34,938 --> 00:00:38,960
And one of the FTC's own examples involves a hotel room and

17
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:39,642
a funeral.

18
00:00:39,963 --> 00:00:41,219
Hold on to that one.

19
00:00:41,448 --> 00:00:43,096
It explains where this is heading.

20
00:00:43,695 --> 00:00:45,765
Here's how those egg prices were found.

21
00:00:46,073 --> 00:00:51,833
Consumer Reports and Groundwork Collaborative, with More Perfect Union, recruited four hundred

22
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:53,549
and thirty-seven volunteers.

23
00:00:53,854 --> 00:00:58,137
In September twenty twenty-five, they loaded the same basket, at the same

24
00:00:58,137 --> 00:01:01,950
store, at the same moment, and wrote down what they were charged.

25
00:01:02,244 --> 00:01:05,774
Seventy-four percent of the items showed more than one price.

26
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:10,242
For those items, the gap between the lowest and highest price averaged

27
00:01:10,242 --> 00:01:11,218
thirteen percent.

28
00:01:11,451 --> 00:01:13,055
The biggest gap was twenty-three.

29
00:01:13,364 --> 00:01:14,905
Whole baskets moved too.

30
00:01:15,143 --> 00:01:19,091
At one Seattle Safeway, the same basket cost a hundred and fourteen

31
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:24,514
thirty-four, a hundred and nineteen eighty-five, or a hundred and twenty-three ninety-three.

32
00:01:24,847 --> 00:01:27,390
That works out to about seven percent per basket.

33
00:01:27,694 --> 00:01:32,025
The researchers applied it to Instacart's own estimate of what a family

34
00:01:32,025 --> 00:01:34,343
of four spends on groceries in a year.

35
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:36,673
Roughly twelve hundred dollars a year.

36
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:39,677
That's a projection, not anyone's actual bill.

37
00:01:39,923 --> 00:01:42,254
And here's the part most of the headlines skipped.

38
00:01:42,754 --> 00:01:46,773
Instacart said the tests were randomized, and that they don't use any

39
00:01:46,773 --> 00:01:48,463
personal or demographic data.

40
00:01:48,728 --> 00:01:53,080
The report itself found differences tied to demographics were small, and not

41
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:54,512
statistically meaningful.

42
00:01:54,810 --> 00:01:57,248
So this wasn't a price built from your profile.

43
00:01:57,593 --> 00:02:01,183
It was a price experiment, and the shoppers were the test subjects.

44
00:02:01,593 --> 00:02:03,623
Nobody told them which group they were in.

45
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:08,574
The tool behind it came from Eversight, a pricing company Instacart bought

46
00:02:08,574 --> 00:02:09,871
in twenty twenty-two.

47
00:02:10,207 --> 00:02:13,902
It tells retailers it can lift sales by one to three percent,

48
00:02:13,902 --> 00:02:15,537
and margins by two to five.

49
00:02:15,937 --> 00:02:19,171
In December, Instacart ended those item price tests.

50
00:02:19,471 --> 00:02:21,339
But the question they raised didn't end.

51
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:24,653
If prices can be tested on you, could they be built for

52
00:02:24,653 --> 00:02:24,863
you?

53
00:02:25,463 --> 00:02:30,121
That's the question the FTC asked eight companies that sell pricing tools

54
00:02:30,121 --> 00:02:32,418
to retailers, in twenty twenty-four.

55
00:02:32,878 --> 00:02:37,457
In January twenty twenty-five, FTC staff published early findings.

56
00:02:37,686 --> 00:02:41,511
As the FTC's chair put it, the data ranged from a person's

57
00:02:41,511 --> 00:02:46,587
location and demographics, quote, down to their mouse movements on a webpage.

58
00:02:47,057 --> 00:02:51,041
Even the products you leave unpurchased in an online shopping cart can

59
00:02:51,041 --> 00:02:51,668
be tracked.

60
00:02:52,008 --> 00:02:56,379
And those intermediaries worked with at least two hundred and fifty client

61
00:02:56,379 --> 00:02:59,568
businesses, from grocery stores to clothing retailers.

62
00:02:59,939 --> 00:03:02,020
And here's why this is so hard to catch.

63
00:03:02,412 --> 00:03:04,746
Online, you only ever see your own price.

64
00:03:05,024 --> 00:03:08,459
There's no shelf next to you showing what the person behind you

65
00:03:08,459 --> 00:03:09,058
was quoted.

66
00:03:09,418 --> 00:03:11,801
The FTC gave one hypothetical.

67
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:16,203
Someone profiled as a new parent could be shown higher priced baby

68
00:03:16,203 --> 00:03:18,846
thermometers on the first page of results.

69
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:20,549
Notice what that is.

70
00:03:20,792 --> 00:03:23,292
Not a different price for the same thermometer.

71
00:03:23,574 --> 00:03:24,546
A different shelf.

72
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:28,459
And that difference, the price versus the placement, is where most of

73
00:03:28,459 --> 00:03:29,483
the myths come from.

74
00:03:30,081 --> 00:03:30,832
Myth one.

75
00:03:31,090 --> 00:03:32,397
Mac users pay more.

76
00:03:32,734 --> 00:03:37,621
What actually happened: in twenty twelve, the Wall Street Journal reported that

77
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:41,888
Orbitz was showing Mac users pricier hotels higher up in the results.

78
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:45,282
Same hotels, same prices, for anyone who looked.

79
00:03:45,629 --> 00:03:47,686
That's steering, not a personal price.

80
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:49,049
Still worth knowing.

81
00:03:49,378 --> 00:03:51,055
Your first page isn't neutral.

82
00:03:51,373 --> 00:03:52,132
Myth two.

83
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:54,983
A low phone battery raises your ride price.

84
00:03:55,215 --> 00:03:59,735
In twenty sixteen, Uber's head of economic research said people with low

85
00:03:59,735 --> 00:04:04,193
battery are more willing to accept surge pricing, and that Uber doesn't

86
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:05,638
use that to set prices.

87
00:04:05,967 --> 00:04:07,548
Now, a documented one.

88
00:04:07,808 --> 00:04:12,206
In twenty nineteen, a TV station in Minneapolis, KARE 11, found that

89
00:04:12,206 --> 00:04:16,992
Target's app showed lower prices outside the store, and switched to higher

90
00:04:16,992 --> 00:04:20,355
in-store prices once the phone was inside the store.

91
00:04:20,687 --> 00:04:24,584
Ten items they checked were two hundred and sixty-two dollars cheaper in

92
00:04:24,584 --> 00:04:26,425
the parking lot than in the store.

93
00:04:26,781 --> 00:04:30,730
Target said it showed in-store prices in stores, and it added labels

94
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:32,356
saying which price is which.

95
00:04:32,744 --> 00:04:33,936
Documented, again.

96
00:04:34,241 --> 00:04:40,338
In twenty fourteen, Northeastern University researchers tested sixteen big shopping and travel

97
00:04:40,338 --> 00:04:44,620
sites, and found price steering or discrimination on nine of them.

98
00:04:45,029 --> 00:04:45,900
And airlines?

99
00:04:46,303 --> 00:04:50,876
In twenty twenty-five, Delta said a company called Fetcherr was helping set

100
00:04:50,876 --> 00:04:54,106
fares on about three percent of its domestic network.

101
00:04:54,444 --> 00:04:56,832
Senators asked if that meant personal fares.

102
00:04:57,143 --> 00:05:01,266
Delta said it has no plans to set individual fares from personal

103
00:05:01,266 --> 00:05:01,588
data.

104
00:05:01,925 --> 00:05:03,562
So here's the honest scoreboard.

105
00:05:03,841 --> 00:05:07,462
A price built from your personal profile is rare, and hard to

106
00:05:07,462 --> 00:05:07,818
prove.

107
00:05:08,197 --> 00:05:11,760
Prices that change with where you are, what device you're on, and

108
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,459
which test group you landed in?

109
00:05:13,939 --> 00:05:15,221
Those are documented.

110
00:05:15,822 --> 00:05:19,763
Which is why the FTC document from August nineteenth matters.

111
00:05:20,032 --> 00:05:24,233
It's a proposed enforcement policy statement on personalized pricing.

112
00:05:24,605 --> 00:05:26,408
It doesn't ban personal prices.

113
00:05:26,744 --> 00:05:27,330
Quote.

114
00:05:27,775 --> 00:05:32,775
Congress has not given the Commission the authority to prohibit personalized pricing

115
00:05:32,775 --> 00:05:33,311
outright.

116
00:05:33,672 --> 00:05:37,204
But where you'd reasonably expect one price for everyone, it says the

117
00:05:37,204 --> 00:05:39,047
seller should tell you three things.

118
00:05:39,442 --> 00:05:41,153
That the price is personalized.

119
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:42,606
The basis for it.

120
00:05:42,883 --> 00:05:44,340
And the type of data used.

121
00:05:44,744 --> 00:05:48,333
Skip that, and the FTC says it's likely to be an unfair

122
00:05:48,333 --> 00:05:49,769
or deceptive practice.

123
00:05:50,259 --> 00:05:51,704
Then come the examples.

124
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:56,428
A grocery chain charging more for milk because data shows several children

125
00:05:56,428 --> 00:05:57,710
live in the household.

126
00:05:57,953 --> 00:06:02,449
A rideshare app charging more because you haven't installed its competitors' apps.

127
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:04,085
And the one I promised.

128
00:06:04,388 --> 00:06:08,254
A hotel charging a higher price because data leads it to believe

129
00:06:08,254 --> 00:06:12,784
you're traveling for a funeral, or some other can't-miss personal business.

130
00:06:13,384 --> 00:06:16,959
That's the whole logic of a personal price, in one sentence.

131
00:06:17,331 --> 00:06:18,567
It doesn't price the room.

132
00:06:18,817 --> 00:06:20,860
It prices how badly you need the room.

133
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:22,580
It's still a proposal.

134
00:06:22,896 --> 00:06:25,014
Comments closed on September twenty-fifth.

135
00:06:25,242 --> 00:06:28,961
And while Washington writes guidance, states have already written laws.

136
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:33,981
Since November twenty twenty-five, New York requires a line next to any

137
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:36,762
algorithmic price built from personal data.

138
00:06:37,115 --> 00:06:38,286
In capital letters.

139
00:06:38,577 --> 00:06:42,527
Fines run up to a thousand dollars per violation, and a federal

140
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:45,911
judge turned down retailers' attempt to block the law.

141
00:06:46,389 --> 00:06:51,886
And this year, Connecticut, Maryland and New Jersey passed laws restricting surveillance

142
00:06:51,886 --> 00:06:54,884
pricing, mostly for groceries and food delivery.

143
00:06:55,257 --> 00:06:57,973
At least eleven more states have bills on the table.

144
00:06:58,572 --> 00:06:59,897
So what can you do today?

145
00:07:00,351 --> 00:07:02,043
Four checks, about two minutes.

146
00:07:02,437 --> 00:07:02,923
One.

147
00:07:03,205 --> 00:07:04,308
Compare logged out.

148
00:07:04,668 --> 00:07:08,433
Open the same item signed out, in a private window, or on

149
00:07:08,433 --> 00:07:09,489
a second device.

150
00:07:09,828 --> 00:07:11,137
A gap tells you something.

151
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:15,498
Private mode won't hide your location, so if it matters, compare at

152
00:07:15,498 --> 00:07:16,600
home and on the go.

153
00:07:17,057 --> 00:07:17,608
Two.

154
00:07:17,944 --> 00:07:19,620
Read the label next to the price.

155
00:07:19,931 --> 00:07:21,460
Online versus in-store.

156
00:07:21,848 --> 00:07:24,588
And in New York, look for that algorithm sentence.

157
00:07:24,954 --> 00:07:25,545
Three.

158
00:07:25,954 --> 00:07:28,192
Compare the final total, not the tag.

159
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:33,328
In the Instacart study, some sale prices matched, but the crossed-out original

160
00:07:33,328 --> 00:07:34,193
prices didn't.

161
00:07:34,562 --> 00:07:35,141
Four.

162
00:07:35,494 --> 00:07:36,783
Cut what the app knows.

163
00:07:37,016 --> 00:07:41,185
Set shopping apps to use location only while open, and use the

164
00:07:41,185 --> 00:07:44,278
opt out links many state privacy laws require.

165
00:07:44,581 --> 00:07:48,491
Rules here are still changing, so check the sources in the description.

166
00:07:48,852 --> 00:07:50,552
This isn't financial advice.

167
00:07:51,153 --> 00:07:53,908
A price used to be a number on a shelf, the same

168
00:07:53,908 --> 00:07:55,286
for whoever walked past.

169
00:07:55,636 --> 00:07:57,575
Online, it can be a guess about you.

170
00:07:57,994 --> 00:08:00,298
The new rules are starting to say one thing.

171
00:08:00,682 --> 00:08:02,028
If you're guessing, say so.

172
00:08:02,321 --> 00:08:05,664
Have you ever seen two different prices for the same thing, on

173
00:08:05,664 --> 00:08:06,258
two phones?

174
00:08:06,655 --> 00:08:08,222
Tell me what it was in the comments.

175
00:08:08,516 --> 00:08:11,505
And if you want to know which rule is deciding your next

176
00:08:11,505 --> 00:08:13,266
price, subscribe to Hidden Layer.
